    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d43">

                <pb facs="0220" n="88" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg88"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Potsdam/6–645</note>
                <head>No. 43 <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic"> Marshal Stalin to Prime Minister Churchill </hi><anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d43-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation?]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-05-30">May 30, 1945</date>.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d43-f2"
                        />]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>For Prime Minister Churchill from Stalin:</p>
                <p>I have received your message of May 29th.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d43-f3"/> A few
                    hours after I had received it Mr. Hopkins was with me and told me that President
                        <pb facs="0221" n="89" xml:id="pg89"/> Truman thinks the most convenient
                    date for a meeting of the three would be June 15th.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d43-f4"
                    /> I have no objection to that date if you also agree to it.</p>
                <p>I send you my best wishes.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d43-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d43-f1-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy of a courtesy copy received by the American Embassy at Moscow
                    on June 1.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d43-f2" n="2" xml:id="d43-f2-content">
                <p>So dated in Churchill, <hi rendition="#italic">Triumph and Tragedy</hi>, p. 581,
                    and in <hi rendition="#italic"> Stalin’s Correspondence</hi>, vol. <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 362.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d43-f3" n="3" xml:id="d43-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d39">document No. 39</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d43-f4" n="4" xml:id="d43-f4-content">
                <p>The following note by Charles E. Bohlen, dated June 1, is typed on the source
                    copy: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I called Mr. Pavlov to verify the question of the date of the meeting.
                            He confirmed that both Marshal Stalin and Mr. Molotov and himself had
                            clearly understood that the President desired it around the middle of
                            July.</p>
                        <p>“After verifying the text of Marshal Stalin’s message to Mr. Churchill,
                            Mr. Pavlov telephoned back to say that in the original there was no
                            mistake and the date had been clearly written July 15th and not repeat
                            not June.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>The correct date was in the message as received by Churchill. See Churchill, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Triumph and Tragedy</hi>, p. 581.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
